  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday... \n\nWe had our one nice, pleasant day with afternoon sunshine boosting \ntemperatures into the mid 70s across the region under a weak ridge \nof high pressure. However, gloomy weather is set to to return the \nthe mid Missouri Valley tonight and likely persist through Saturday. \nThe first in a series of shortwaves is currently approaching from \nwest, and will reignite showers across the region tonight. While a \nfew rumbles of thunder can't be ruled out, overall instability will \nremain weak at best through Saturday. Substantial Gulf moisture will \nremain over the Central CONUS, with PWATs rising to 1.5\"-2\". LREF \nprobs estimate a 50-70% chance of seeing more than 1.5\" through \nSaturday and a 30-45% chance of seeing greater than 2\" of rain.  \n\nPrecipitation chances wind down Saturday afternoon and evening, \nending from west to east. Temperatures Saturday afternoon will be \ndependent on how quickly cloud cover clears out of the region. If \nafternoon sun manages to peak through, highs will likely reach into \nthe mid 70s. However if cloud cover prevails, look to temperatures \nto remain similar to Thursday and Friday, in the 60s to lower 70s. \n\nSunday and Beyond...\n\nAnother brief ridge will build into the region Sunday, giving us \nanother glimpse of sunshine and dry weather. Afternoon highs will \nlikely reach the 70s. \n\nMidlevel thermal ridging ahead of the next trough and surface cold \nfront will allow temperatures to remain in the 70s on Monday, before \nshowers and storms return to the region. Longer range models \ncontinue to hint at the potential for strong moisture advection into \nthe region with this next system Monday through Wednesday, \npotentially supplemented by moisture from Hurricane Polo off the \nwestern coast of Mexico. Given the well-saturated soil conditions \nand potential for another round of heavy, abundant rainfall, areas \nof flooding may very well be a possibility. In addition to \npotentially heavy rain, a more unstable and sheared air mass could \nlead to the formation of strong to severe storms, as is highlighted \nby a 5-10% probability in machine learning guidance.",
